1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,652
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,711 --> 00:00:09,851
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:09,851 --> 00:00:10,559
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,618 --> 00:00:13,920
Welcome to Unit 6: Chemical Reactivity.

5
00:00:14,979 --> 00:00:18,342
Car exhausts release harmful gases like carbon monoxide.

6
00:00:18,342 --> 00:00:23,358
Catalytic converters use rare, expensive metals to turn them into less harmful ones.

7
00:00:23,358 --> 00:00:28,374
Why do some metals react so easily, while others hardly react at all? Let's find out.

8
00:00:29,433 --> 00:00:33,387
Strips of magnesium, copper and gold are each held in a hot flame.

9
00:00:33,387 --> 00:00:39,701
Predict: which metal will react most strongly with the oxygen in the air, and which will not react at all?

10
00:00:39,701 --> 00:00:40,704
Pause and decide.

11
00:00:41,763 --> 00:00:45,126
Watch. Each metal is held in the flame for the same time.

12
00:00:46,185 --> 00:00:51,378
The magnesium burns with a dazzling white flame. The copper turns black on the surface.

13
00:00:51,378 --> 00:00:52,853
The gold does not change.

14
00:00:53,912 --> 00:00:59,282
Explain what you saw. Magnesium is very reactive: it burns, forming white magnesium oxide.

15
00:00:59,282 --> 00:01:03,885
Copper is less reactive: only its surface reacts, forming black copper oxide.

16
00:01:03,885 --> 00:01:10,140
Gold is unreactive and does not react with oxygen, even when heated. Metal plus oxygen gives metal oxide.

17
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:12,441
Pause and explain it in your own words.

18
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:18,811
Most metals react with oxygen to form a metal oxide: metal plus oxygen gives metal oxide.

19
00:01:18,811 --> 00:01:21,939
But how fast they react depends on their reactivity.

20
00:01:21,939 --> 00:01:28,252
Potassium and sodium react so violently with oxygen that they're stored in oil, to keep air away from them.

21
00:01:29,312 --> 00:01:33,029
Calcium and magnesium are less reactive, but still react well.

22
00:01:33,029 --> 00:01:37,691
Magnesium burns in oxygen with a dazzling white flame, forming magnesium oxide.

23
00:01:38,750 --> 00:01:43,057
Iron and zinc react slowly with oxygen. That's why rust forms over time.

24
00:01:43,057 --> 00:01:48,486
Copper is even less reactive: it only reacts when heated, slowly forming black copper oxide.

25
00:01:49,545 --> 00:01:53,027
Silver and gold don't react with oxygen, even when heated.

26
00:01:53,027 --> 00:01:58,515
They keep their shine for a very long time, which makes them perfect for jewellery and coins.

27
00:01:59,574 --> 00:02:05,652
Iron isn't found pure in nature. It's in rocks called ores, like haematite, which contains iron oxide.

28
00:02:05,652 --> 00:02:09,959
In a blast furnace, the ore reacts with carbon monoxide to produce iron,

29
00:02:09,959 --> 00:02:13,500
but this releases a lot of carbon dioxide, a greenhouse gas.

30
00:02:14,559 --> 00:02:21,522
Another method is electrolysis: passing an electric current through molten iron oxide splits it into iron and oxygen.

31
00:02:21,522 --> 00:02:24,472
It's more expensive and uses lots of electricity,

32
00:02:24,472 --> 00:02:29,311
so it's only more sustainable when that electricity comes from renewable sources.

33
00:02:29,311 --> 00:02:34,327
Very reactive metals like sodium and potassium can only be extracted by electrolysis.
